From b17bdcbe6747ef6f7ea260c3b14233ea9d003f3c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dennis Berger <dbgh@knownhosts.org>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 11:50:05 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] test(engine): Add property tests for the settlement loop
 (no-changelog)

`settlement.ts`, `completion.ts` and `StepSettledHandler` document claims
about the whole settlement loop: fates do not depend on the order of
events, the execution finishes when the settled count reaches
`countExpectedSettledSteps`, a skip cascades one hop for each event, and
any planner at any time recomputes the same decisions. The unit tests
check single decisions. The existing property test checks loopless graphs
on `decideSuccessors` alone.

This test runs `ExecutionStartHandler` and `StepSettledHandler` without
changes, over in-memory stores that follow the `ExecutionStore` and
`StepStore` contracts. A step worker settles each queued step with an
outcome that depends on its step key only. The test delivers the messages
of each scenario in every order. It checks that:

- the fates match a whole-graph evaluation of rules 2 and 3, and are the
  same in every order
- the execution ends `completed` with the settled count equal to
  `countExpectedSettledSteps`, and no step is created after it ends
- every order ends, after one delivery for each step and one for each
  queued step
- a settlement creates only direct successors of its step, and announces
  each one
- in every state reached, planning a settled step again agrees with the
  final fates, and the rows that `decisionKeys` names give the same
  decisions as all rows

Scenarios: every DAG of up to 3 nodes with every output slot pattern, and
every DAG of 4 nodes with the pattern that fills every read slot and one
other seeded pattern (1,696 graphs, 4,160 scenarios, 88,115 delivery
orders). 48 loop graphs (1,152 scenarios, 8,892 orders). 150 random
graphs, in 20 random orders each, with redelivered messages: 53 DAGs, 48
with one loop and 49 with two loops. 222 scenarios in which one step
fails, where the execution must end `failed` with every step settled.
The test pins the scenario counts and the count of each random kind, so
a generator that shrinks or stops making a kind fails.

The random generators seed the PRNG from a hash of a label. With integer
seeds 1 to 150, the first draw of the LCG lies in [0.23, 0.30], so a
first draw of `randomInt(rand, 3)` is always 0.

Each delivery runs alone, so the test does not cover concurrent handlers.
The file runs in about 2 to 4 seconds. Each test has a 60 s timeout,
because the default 5 s is too short on a loaded CI machine. The test
adds no dependency and changes no source file.
